      <h1>Readwise Books Challenges Kindle: EPUB Support &amp; Migration Guide (2026)</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-10-31">Published October 31, 2026</time></p>

      <p>For more than a decade, the ebook market has been a duopoly: Amazon's Kindle on one side and Rakuten's Kobo on the other. In October 2026, Readwise — the company best known for its read-later and spaced-repetition tools — launched <strong>Readwise Books</strong>, a full-fledged ebook platform that aims to break that duopoly by making your EPUB library portable, social, and sync-first. Unlike Kindle, Readwise Books doesn't lock you into a proprietary format or a single vendor's cloud. It reads EPUB natively, syncs your highlights to its signature review system, and even runs on your own devices without phoning home.</p>

      <p>The launch has been covered by <a href="https://www.theverge.com/">The Verge</a>, <a href="https://overkill.wtf/readwise-books/">overkill.wtf</a>, and a handful of ebook bloggers who have been waiting years for a credible Kindle alternative. This article breaks down what Readwise Books actually offers for EPUB readers, how its format support compares to Kindle and Kobo, and how to migrate your existing library if you're ready to switch.</p>

      <h2>What Is Readwise Books?</h2>

      <p>Readwise Books is the company's attempt to solve a problem its users have complained about for years: they use Readwise to capture and review highlights from ebooks, but the ebooks themselves live in silos. Kindle books are locked to Amazon's ecosystem. Kobo books are locked to Rakuten's. Even DRM-free purchases from independent stores often require side-loading or manual conversion. Readwise Books is designed to be the "third way" — a platform where you bring your own EPUBs, annotate them freely, and sync everything to Readwise's review pipeline without any vendor lock-in.</p>

      <p>The core pitch is simple: <strong>your books, your highlights, your data</strong>. Readwise Books stores your EPUBs in a cloud library (encrypted at rest), lets you read them in a web app or on dedicated mobile apps, and syncs your annotations back to Readwise where they feed into the daily review emails and spaced-repetition flashcards that the company is known for. There's no proprietary format, no DRM wrapper, and no restriction on exporting your annotations.</p>

      <h2>EPUB Support: The Killer Feature</h2>

      <p>Unlike Kindle, which has historically resisted EPUB in favor of its proprietary AZW3/KFX formats, Readwise Books treats EPUB as its native format. You can upload any DRM-free EPUB file directly to your library, and the platform handles it without conversion. This is a significant departure from the status quo. If you've been using tools like our <a href="/blog/epub-to-kindle-formatting-2026/">EPUB-to-Kindle formatting guide</a> or Calibre to convert EPUBs for Kindle compatibility, Readwise Books eliminates that step entirely.</p>

      <p>The platform also supports a few other formats for import, but EPUB is the clear first-class citizen. Uploads are fast, the web reader renders EPUB 3 features like fixed-layout spreads and embedded fonts correctly, and the annotation layer (highlights, notes, tags) is built directly into the reading experience. For readers who have been collecting DRM-free EPUBs from publishers like <a href="https://standardbooks.org/">Standard Ebooks</a>, <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/">Project Gutenberg</a>, or direct-from-author stores, Readwise Books is the first major platform that treats your library as yours rather than as a licensed rental.</p>

